================================================================================
PART 5 -- PROVING EMP BY LAPLACE ASYMPTOTICS (not just degree-by-degree)
================================================================================

 CLAIM.  h in C[r] of degree d >= 1, leading coeff c_d, next coeff c_{d-1}.  Then

     E[h(r)^m]  =  c_d^m (dm)! exp( c_{d-1} / (c_d d) ) (1 + o(1))    as m -> infinity.

 REASON.  E[h^m] = int_0^inf e^{-r} h(r)^m dr.  Write h(r) = c_d r^d phi(r) with
 phi(r) = 1 + (c_{d-1}/c_d)/r + O(1/r^2), so beyond the largest root phi is nonvanishing.
 The integrand e^{-r} r^{dm} peaks at the saddle r = dm, and THERE

     m log phi(r) = m [ (c_{d-1}/c_d)/r + O(1/r^2) ] -> c_{d-1}/(c_d d),

 a CONSTANT -- the subleading coefficients contribute a finite factor, not a growing one.
 The phase arg h(r) -> arg c_d as r -> infinity, so it is asymptotically CONSTANT across the
 peak: there is no oscillation to cancel the integral.  Hence

     E[h^m] ~ c_d^m e^{c_{d-1}/(c_d d)} int_0^inf e^{-r} r^{dm} dr = c_d^m e^{...} (dm)!.

 The amplitude is NONZERO, so E[h^m] != 0 for all large m.  Therefore E[h^m] = 0 for every
 m >= 1 forces h constant, and then E[h] = h = 0.  THAT IS EMP, for every degree.

 NUMERICAL TEST OF THE ASYMPTOTIC (the ratio must -> exp(c_{d-1}/(c_d d)) ):

  h = r^2 + (-2+0.5i) r + (3+i)
    predicted limit exp(c_(d-1)/(c_d d)) = 0.356443+0.091015j
      m=  10  E[h^m]/(c_d^m (dm)!) =     0.372240    +0.110971i   |ratio-target| = 2.545e-02
      m=  20  E[h^m]/(c_d^m (dm)!) =     0.364130    +0.100485i   |ratio-target| = 1.220e-02
      m=  40  E[h^m]/(c_d^m (dm)!) =     0.360235    +0.095636i   |ratio-target| = 5.978e-03
      m=  80  E[h^m]/(c_d^m (dm)!) =     0.358326    +0.093298i   |ratio-target| = 2.960e-03
