 The error HALVES each time m doubles -- the O(1/m) decay of a Laplace expansion, which is
 exactly what the saddle-point derivation predicts.  The limit is NONZERO in every case,
 including for the exact degree-2 root that kills E[h] and E[h^2]: that polynomial cannot
 keep killing moments, because the asymptotic amplitude does not vanish.  EMP is PROVED.

================================================================================
PART 6 -- NC2, HENCE GMC(2), FOR EVERY TWO-WEIGHT P, IN EVERY DEGREE
================================================================================

 P supported on exactly two weights p < q.  Wick keeps total weight 0, so in P^m only terms
 with i p + j q = 0, i + j = m survive.
   (i)  0 < p < q (or p < q < 0): no solution with m >= 1 -- every moment vanishes for FREE.
        These are precisely the members NC2 permits.
   (ii) p = 0 < q: forces j = 0, so E[P^m] = E_r[f(r)^m] with f the weight-0 part.  EMP => f = 0.
   (iii) p < 0 < q: i|p| = j q with i+j = m pins (i,j), and f^i g^j = (f^q g^{|p|})^{m/(q+|p|)}
        is a POWER, so E[P^m] = C(m,i) E_r[H^k].  EMP => H = 0 => f = 0 or g = 0.
 In (ii) and (iii) P collapses to a single weight.  So NC2 holds on every two-weight stratum,
 and with Part 3's implication:   GMC(2) HOLDS FOR EVERY P SUPPORTED ON AT MOST TWO WEIGHTS.

================================================================================
PART 7 -- THE FIRST UNCOVERED STRATUM IS {-1,0,1} -- the shape of the n>=3 witnesses
================================================================================
   P = Zb a(r) + b(r) + Z c(r), a,b,c of degree <= 1, coeffs in {0,+-1,+-2,+-i}
   triples tested: 117648;   NC2 violations: 0
   NONE -- consistent with NC2 (bounded box, NOT a proof)

 Bessel form of this stratum, from the polar decomposition (r ~ Exp(1) _|_ theta ~ Unif):
     sum_m E[P^m] t^m / m!  =  E_r[ e^{b(r) t} I_0( 2 t sqrt( r a(r) c(r) ) ) ]
 and GMC(2) here is the claim that this equals 1 only when r a c = 0 and b = 0.  THAT is the
 precise remaining gap: a Bessel-weighted analogue of EMP.  The same saddle-point method
 should apply -- I_0(x) ~ e^x/sqrt(2 pi x) has no oscillation on the positive axis either --
 but I have not carried it through, and I am not claiming it.

